\description{
Reads an \href{https://icechunk.io}{Icechunk} repository at a branch, tag or
snapshot through the zarrs Rust backend. The store is read-only.
}
\details{
\code{IcechunkStore} needs the zarrs backend built with the \code{icechunk}
feature; check with \code{"icechunk" \%in\% pizzarr_compiled_features()}. There
is no pure-R fallback, so on other builds \verb{$new()} raises an error.

Creating the store opens the repository and a read-only session. A
branch is resolved to its tip at that moment; create a new store to see
later commits. Array reads go through the zarrs fast path. Metadata and
any selection the fast path does not handle are read key by key through
the same session.

\code{url} is a local path, or an \verb{s3://}, \verb{gs://}, \verb{az://} or \verb{https://}
URL. \code{storage_options} is a named list. All entries are optional:

\describe{
\item{\code{anonymous}}{\code{TRUE} for unsigned requests to a public bucket.}
\item{\code{region}, \code{endpoint_url}, \code{allow_http}, \code{force_path_style}}{S3
client settings. \code{endpoint_url} selects an S3-compatible service such
as MinIO; for GCS it sets the base URL.}
\item{\code{access_key_id}, \code{secret_access_key}, \code{session_token}}{Static S3
credentials. Without these or \code{anonymous}, credentials come from the
environment (\verb{AWS_*} variables, profiles, instance metadata).}
\item{\code{service_account_file}, \code{service_account_key},
\code{application_credentials}, \code{bearer_token}}{Static GCS credentials.
Without these, credentials come from the environment.}
\item{\code{account}, \code{access_key}, \code{sas_token}, \code{bearer_token}}{Azure
account (required for \verb{az://}) and static credentials.}
}

Repositories with virtual chunks may only read from locations you
authorize. \code{virtual_chunk_containers} is a named list whose names are URL
prefixes ending in \code{/} (\code{"s3://bucket/data/"}, \code{"file:///data/"},
\code{"https://host/path/"}) and whose values are option lists, as for
\code{storage_options}. A prefix the repository does not declare is added as
a new container. Give a \code{name} to point the repository's named container
(used by relative \verb{vcc://name/...} references) at this prefix.

Credentials that expire and need refreshing are not supported.
}
